finding: 1. src/cli/publish-fly.ts:160–219,238–250 — The preflight does not distinguish a Fly Launch Machine from an unmanaged Machines API Machine. A hand-linked app with one unmanaged shared-1x/256 MB Machine and the exact expected attached volume passes. Pinned flyctl v0.5.12 deploy/machines.go:360–411 lists Fly Launch Machines separately and marks a deploy first when there are active Machines but no Launch Machines; :321 provisions before subsequent volume validation. machines.go:539–553 excludes attached volumes from the available pool, so deploy_first.go:123–185 creates another volume for the generated mount. --ha=false limits the new process group's replicas, not total app inventory. The passing fake inventory has no management metadata and the fake deploy cannot exhibit this behavior. 2. src/cli/publish-fly.ts:233–250; src/cli/publish.ts:547–570,595–597 — Failed or interrupted first publish can strand a billable volume. Pinned flyctl creates it before release/Machine completion, with no Shallot rollback. Even a successful server deploy followed by a web build/upload failure leaves the app and volume before the first local link is saved; retrying with the same name can refuse as an existing first-link app. The outer finally only removes local work; deletion guidance is printed after client deployment succeeds. 3. src/cli/publish-fly.ts:196–219,293–297 — A matching pre-existing volume with scheduled snapshots enabled is admitted. Pinned flyctl uses ScheduledSnapshots only in its first-deploy CreateVolume request (deploy_first.go:182); generated TOML does not turn backups off on the existing volume. The redeploy spend guard therefore does not enforce the snapshots-off storage envelope.
